<blockquote>
<p><strong>Lead-in:</strong>
Whether you participate in DAOs (decentralized autonomous organizations), manage remote teams with digital tools, or are simply curious about &quot;what's left for humans in the AI era,&quot; these three papers will offer you a fresh perspective.</p>
<p>Each answers a key question: <strong>When organizations no longer rely on traditional hierarchies, what drives people to work, and what drains them?</strong></p>
</blockquote>

<hr />

<h2>Paper 1: Token-Centric Work Practices in Fluid Organizations</h2>
<h3>When Organizations Become &quot;Fluid,&quot; Tokens Become the New Management Tool</h3>

<p><em>Schirrmacher, N. B., Jensen, J. R., &amp; Avital, M. (2021)</em></p>

<h3>Core Question</h3>
<p>Traditional enterprises have org charts, management layers, and policies. But what if an organization has <strong>no fixed structure, no employment relationships, and not even an office — relying entirely on strangers collaborating via smart contracts on a blockchain?</strong></p>
<p>This is the reality of &quot;fluid organizations&quot; — a new, radically flexible organizational form. Two of the most representative examples are Yearn Finance and MakerDAO.</p>

<h3>What They Found</h3>
<p>The researchers found that in such organizations, <strong>tokens serve multiple functions that in traditional organizations would be handled by management, HR, finance, and even corporate culture combined</strong>:</p>
<ol>
  <li><strong>Coordination Tool</strong>: Tokens are not money — they are signals. A token holder's voting weight determines project direction, aligning decisions across people dispersed worldwide</li>
  <li><strong>Incentive Engine</strong>: Token value is tied to overall organizational success — every contribution makes everyone's tokens more valuable, creating a scaled-up version of &quot;everyone is a shareholder&quot;</li>
  <li><strong>Governance Gateway</strong>: Token holders can vote on fund allocation, protocol parameters, and even who gets hired</li>
  <li><strong>Identity Marker</strong>: In an environment with no face-to-face meetings or badges, the number of tokens you hold is your &quot;presence&quot; in the organization</li>
</ol>

<h3>Why It Matters</h3>
<p>This paper was published in 2021. Back then, most people considered DAOs a niche crypto enthusiast toy. But five years later, <strong>the logic of &quot;tokenized collaboration&quot; is seeping into traditional enterprises at scale</strong>:</p>
<ul>
  <li>Remote teams use &quot;reputation points&quot; to manage contributions</li>
  <li>Open-source projects use &quot;contribution tokens&quot; to distribute revenue</li>
  <li>Creator communities use NFTs as identity and权益 tokens</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>In a word: Tokens are not digital currency — they are a new kind of organizational management tool. Understanding them is understanding the foundational architecture of future work.</strong></p>

<hr />

<h2>Paper 2: Tedious Work</h2>
<h3>Digital Technology Makes Your Work More &quot;Tedious&quot; — And That's the Most Dangerous Part</h3>

<p><em>Bruns, H. C., &amp; Long Lingo, E. (2024)</em><br />
<em>Administrative Science Quarterly, 69(1), 39-79</em></p>

<h3>Core Question</h3>
<p>Digital technology dramatically reduces the iteration cost and modification convenience of creative work. This sounds like all good news. But Bruns and Long Lingo ask a counterintuitive question: <strong>When &quot;doing it over&quot; becomes too easy, can we get trapped in the iteration itself?</strong></p>

<h3>What They Found</h3>
<p>The researchers conducted comparative ethnographic studies in two very different fields — systems biology labs and music production studios. They identified a widely overlooked problem: <strong>&quot;tedious work.&quot;</strong></p>
<p>Definition: <strong>Repetitive, detail-oriented work that requires domain expertise but lacks creativity.</strong></p>
<p>They identified four types:</p>
<ol>
  <li><strong>&quot;Fishing&quot;</strong>: Searching through vast amounts of data for that one potentially meaningful clue — not exploring, but &quot;fishing&quot;</li>
  <li><strong>&quot;Administrating&quot;</strong>: Managing versions, labeling files, organizing metadata — 20% creating, 80% organizing</li>
  <li><strong>&quot;Polishing&quot;</strong>: Fine-tuning a parameter, adjusting a track's volume, tweaking a page margin — not changing essence but pursuing perfection</li>
  <li><strong>&quot;Compiling&quot;</strong>: Piecing together outputs from different sources and making sure they work together — the tedium of integration</li>
</ol>

<h3>The Three &quot;Silent Killers&quot; of Tedious Work</h3>
<p>Digital technology enables infinite iteration at every step, but it also creates three traps:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Time Sink</strong>: You spend 80% of your time on repeated modifications</li>
  <li><strong>Motivation Drain</strong>: Repetitive operations exhaust your intrinsic drive</li>
  <li><strong>Information Overload</strong>: Too many versions, you can't remember which is the final one</li>
</ul>

<h3>Why It Matters</h3>
<p>This paper is profoundly significant for &quot;AI-era creators.&quot;</p>
<p><strong>Its key lesson: AI should not only help you &quot;create faster&quot; but should also help you reduce &quot;tedious work.&quot;</strong></p>
<p>If you use AI to assist with creation (writing, coding, design, research), an important question to ask is: <strong>Is AI reducing your tedious work, or is it just helping you generate more tedious work faster?</strong></p>
<p>A good assistant should let you focus on &quot;fishing&quot; and &quot;exploration,&quot; not help you &quot;polish&quot; and &quot;compile&quot; things that may have no meaning at 10x speed.</p>
<p><strong>In a word: The freedom to iterate comes at a cost. Digital technology opens the door to infinite revisions, but only those who know when to stop and channel their energy into what truly matters can produce genuinely impactful outcomes.</strong></p>

<hr />

<h2>Paper 3: Beyond Money</h2>
<h3>No Cash, Just &quot;Shares&quot;: The Incentive Experiment in DAO Communities</h3>

<p><em>Chen, K., Fan, Y., Fang, Y., &amp; Luo, X. (2025)</em><br />
<em>Journal of Operations Management, 71(7), 988-1016</em></p>

<h3>Core Question</h3>
<p>If you run a community (whether a DAO, open-source project, or creator community), do you want members to create more content or maintain more order? How much incentive is enough? What happens if you give &quot;shares&quot; (token ownership) instead of &quot;cash&quot;?</p>

<h3>How They Studied It</h3>
<p>The researchers analyzed real behavioral data from 98,000 users on Steemit (a blockchain-based social media platform) from 2017 to 2019.</p>
<p>Steemit's unique feature: it has two types of tokens:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Steem</strong> (tradeable token) — convertible to cash, equivalent to &quot;salary&quot;</li>
  <li><strong>Steem Power</strong> (governance token) — not directly redeemable, but carries more voting power, similar to &quot;equity&quot;</li>
</ul>
<p>The &quot;Power-up&quot; operation — where users choose to convert Steem into Steem Power (swapping cash for equity) — served as a natural experiment for the researchers to observe the effects of &quot;ownership incentives.&quot;</p>

<h3>What They Found</h3>
<p><strong>1. Ownership (equity) vs. Cash (salary) have very different effects</strong></p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr><th>Dimension</th><th>Cash (tradeable token) driven</th><th>Equity (governance token) driven</th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Content creation volume</td><td>Moderate ↑</td><td><strong>Significant ↑ ↑</strong></td></tr>
<tr><td>Maintenance behavior (likes, comments)</td><td>Moderate ↑</td><td><strong>Strong ↑ ↑ ↑</strong></td></tr>
<tr><td>Content novelty</td><td>Normal</td><td><strong>↓ Decreased</strong></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<p><strong>2. Interesting counterintuitive findings</strong></p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Equity incentives make you more willing to &quot;maintain the community&quot; rather than &quot;create new content&quot;</strong>: Users with ownership were more inclined to like and comment on others' content (curation) rather than posting their own</li>
  <li><strong>Equity incentives reduce content novelty</strong>: Users tended to write &quot;safe, popular&quot; content rather than take creative risks</li>
  <li><strong>The effect of equity incentives diminishes over time</strong>: Once the novelty wears off, so does the effect</li>
  <li><strong>But if you let users reconfirm their choice (e.g., &quot;power-up&quot; again), the effect recovers</strong></li>
  <li><strong>Users with weak social ties are more easily motivated by equity incentives</strong>: Newcomers need a greater sense of &quot;ownership&quot;</li>
  <li><strong>High-reputation users are less responsive to equity incentives</strong>: They are already creating for other reasons</li>
</ul>

<h3>Why It Matters</h3>
<p>This has direct implications for any &quot;incentive-driven platform business&quot;:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Free Diagnosis → Paid Service: What Is Your &quot;Equity&quot;?</strong>
In our business, free diagnosis is a &quot;tradeable token&quot; (usable immediately but expires quickly), while subscriptions or 1-on-1 consultations are the &quot;governance token&quot; (long-term relationship, growing equity). The former drives short-term usage, the latter drives deep engagement.</li>
  <li><strong>Warning on Novelty Decline</strong>: If users receive too much &quot;equity-style&quot; return, they may become conservative. This is especially important in educational contexts — if students' motivation is overdriven by &quot;point rankings,&quot; exploratory learning may be suppressed.</li>
  <li><strong>Re-affirmation Effect</strong>: Periodically letting users &quot;confirm&quot; their choice (e.g., renewing, re-subscribing) can reactivate the incentive effect.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>In a word: Equity incentives make people more loyal but more conservative; cash incentives make people freer but more detached. When designing incentives, consider not &quot;what others would choose,&quot; but &quot;what kind of contributor do you want your participants to become.&quot;</strong></p>

<hr />

<h2>Putting the Three Papers Together: The Bigger Picture</h2>

<table>
<thead>
<tr><th>Dimension</th><th>Paper 1 (Fluid Orgs)</th><th>Paper 2 (Tedious Work)</th><th>Paper 3 (Token Incentives)</th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Core concern</td><td>How to coordinate without structure</td><td>How digital tools drain creativity</td><td>How incentive design shapes behavior</td></tr>
<tr><td>Shared theme</td><td colspan="3">Digital technology is redefining &quot;work&quot; itself</td></tr>
<tr><td>AI-era implications</td><td>Tokens = basic mgmt tool</td><td>AI = tedious work vacuum</td><td>Incentives = behavior shaper</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<p>The shared insight across these three papers is: <strong>Technology is reshaping the fundamental logic of &quot;organizations&quot; and &quot;work&quot; to an extent beyond our expectations.</strong></p>
<ul>
  <li>The traditional &quot;get paid → do work&quot; model is being supplemented — and even replaced — by &quot;tokenized collaboration&quot;</li>
  <li>Digital tools give people creative freedom on one hand, while silently consuming creators' energy and time on the other</li>
  <li>Incentive design is no longer just an economic issue — it is a behavioral psychology issue</li>
</ul>
<p>For those of us building a human-AI collaborative business model, these academic insights are not shelf decorations — <strong>they are the skeleton of product design.</strong></p>

